[
    {
        "content": "<p>I have this code</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Field</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Ring</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ring</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ring</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ring_nf</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>All the three examples compile. But in the second example, I'm getting</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The <code>ring</code> tactic failed to close the goal. Use <code>ring_nf</code> to obtain a normal form.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>probably because the two sides of the equality are wrapped in lists, even though the goal does get closed after all.</p>\n<p>Thought about making the warning not fire in this case? Or is it too specific and not worth the special case?</p>",
        "id": 627041693,
        "sender_full_name": "user202729",
        "timestamp": 1790398288
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In this case, the message is correct, the <code>ring</code> tactic alone indeed fails to close the goal, and the fallback to use <code>ring_nf</code> is triggered (though in this case it happens that <code>ring_nf</code> ends up closing the goal). Instead, you should just use <code>ring_nf</code> directly. You can see that</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Field</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Defs</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Ring</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ring1</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>fails with an error, <code>ring1</code> is a version of <code>ring</code> that doesn't try <code>ring_nf</code> on failure.</p>",
        "id": 627042470,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1790398961
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Okay I see. I suppose the part that confused me is that I thought it normally error out when it cannot close the goal, and that <code>ring</code> only works at top-level but <code>ring_nf</code> works for any subexpressions (given their similar name I would've guessed that the only difference is one is a closing tactic and the other isn't, but that wasn't right)</p>",
        "id": 627043019,
        "sender_full_name": "user202729",
        "timestamp": 1790399471
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Do we still have any good reason for having separate <code>ring</code> and <code>ring_nf</code> tactics? I think the idea was that <code>ring</code> is a bit more efficient, but I doubt that is by a very significant amount.</p>",
        "id": 627056477,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1790409196
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"816123\">user202729</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/.60ring.60.20tactic.20working.20on.20subexpressions.3F/near/627043019\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>(given their similar name I would've guessed that the only difference is one is a closing tactic and the other isn't, but that wasn't right)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I made this mistake too, and find the naming not particularly intuitive based on my understanding of what it does.</p>",
        "id": 627057693,
        "sender_full_name": "Tjeerd Jan Heeringa",
        "timestamp": 1790409703
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thinking about it, maybe one easy improvement would be to change the message to</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Using</strong> <code>ring_nf</code> to obtain a normal form.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So that it's clearer that the info message is saying that the tactic is itself using <code>ring_nf</code> as a fallback, rather than merely suggesting to the user that the code should be changed to use <code>ring_nf</code>.</p>\n<p>(or is the point is to give the suggestion?)</p>",
